// Specifications: the kinds a spec is written in, from std/spec, in its three
// layers, with projects, delivery and how a spec tree is laid out.
include = [
  "./layers.heron",
  "./intent.heron",
  "./context.heron.md",
  "./requirements.heron",
  "./usecases.heron",
  "./contract.heron",
  "./lifecycles.heron",
  "./planning.heron.md",
  "./traceability.heron",
  "./projects.heron",
  "./implementations.heron",
  "./delivery.heron",
  "./quality_checks.heron",
  "./retired.heron",
  "./layout.heron.md",
]

@lens(user, developer)
| The kinds a specification is written in, in three [layers](#heron.language.specifications.layers.layer): the intent, why the product exists and for whom; the specification, what must hold of it from outside; and the contract, the exact surface a consumer relies on. Then the links between them, [lifecycles](#language.specifications.lifecycles.lifecycle), open questions, proof and the gaps it leaves, specs and their implementations, delivery, the checks a spec passes, the [retired kinds](#heron.language.specifications.retired.retired_kind) and how a spec tree is laid out.

specs_traceable: goal(measure = "Every requirement reaches a goal and a proof.") {{
  Teams state what their product must do so that every requirement says why it exists and how it is proven.
}}
